      },
      "worked": "Let $O$ be the circumcentre and $R$ the circumradius. The altitude of the equilateral triangle is $\\frac{\\sqrt3r}{2}$, and its circumcentre lies two-thirds of the way from a vertex along the median.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}R&=\\frac23\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt3r}{2}\\right)\\\\R&=\\frac{r}{\\sqrt3}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe minor angle $\\angle BOC$ is $\\frac{2\\pi}{3}$, twice $\\angle BAC=\\frac{\\pi}{3}$. The overlap consists of the smaller circle’s major segment above $BC$ and the larger circle’s minor segment below $BC$.\n\nThe smaller circle’s major segment is its major sector plus $\\triangle OBC$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}A_{\\text{major}}&=\\frac12\\left(\\frac{r}{\\sqrt3}\\right)^2\\frac{4\\pi}{3}+\\frac12\\left(\\frac{r}{\\sqrt3}\\right)^2\\sin\\frac{2\\pi}{3}\\\\A_{\\text{major}}&=\\frac{2\\pi r^2}{9}+\\frac{\\sqrt3r^2}{12}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe larger circle’s minor segment is its minor sector minus $\\triangle ABC$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}A_{\\text{minor}}&=\\frac12r^2\\left(\\frac{\\pi}{3}-\\sin\\frac{\\pi}{3}\\right)\\\\A_{\\text{minor}}&=\\frac{\\pi r^2}{6}-\\frac{\\sqrt3r^2}{4}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nHence the overlapping area is\n\n$$\\begin{align*}A&=\\frac{2\\pi r^2}{9}+\\frac{\\sqrt3r^2}{12}+\\frac{\\pi r^2}{6}-\\frac{\\sqrt3r^2}{4}\\\\A&=\\frac{7\\pi r^2}{18}-\\frac{\\sqrt3r^2}{6}\\\\A&=\\frac{r^2}{18}\\left(7\\pi-3\\sqrt3\\right).\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
